{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-8715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +0=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=0,\\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-8715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2c8f50623549da6e3317138ce3e1237d8d1e82aef94ff9684c0b617b05175fd"} {"question" "q-8715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-8715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-8715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/assets/f00d8bd6cbd1f113df7f023e161e4d79e2f4a81157d1ac9a8ab65e70b4959221.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2c8f50623549da6e3317138ce3e1237d8d1e82aef94ff9684c0b617b05175fd"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-20414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/assets/6e2223a7dadd9aeddad0b029bf2ba8fc18bafc74dcaca88346ee2b702029b597.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $, hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is  $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2c8f50623549da6e3317138ce3e1237d8d1e82aef94ff9684c0b617b05175fd"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-72829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=8$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ rounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)=82.874\\,983{…}^{∘}≈{82.87}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${82.87}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={82.87}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{82.87}^{∘}={262.87}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2c8f50623549da6e3317138ce3e1237d8d1e82aef94ff9684c0b617b05175fd"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x={63}^{∘},{243}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={70}^{∘},{250}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={76}^{∘},{256}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={88}^{∘},{268}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-121127" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)={0}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+{0}^{∘}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=180\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e2c8f50623549da6e3317138ce3e1237d8d1e82aef94ff9684c0b617b05175fd"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"} {"question" "q-321873" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4f6dd23c10da57a9e61686d8b72ecc366c2b5885896ddb80f95dd4d21c5490b"}] "usage" {"q-20414" []} "basis" 1487 "retractions" [[17592186092569 :quest